{% extends 'shells/docs.html.twig' %}
{% block title %}Deployment · php-via Docs{% endblock %}
{% block content %}
<article>
    <h1>Deployment</h1>
    <p>
        php-via runs as a long-lived <a href="https://openswoole.com/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">OpenSwoole</a> server process.
        Unlike traditional PHP-FPM setups, the process stays running: all state lives in memory.
        This page covers the service, the reverse proxy, several workers, static files, coroutine hooks
        and security settings.
    </p>

    <h2 id="requirements">Requirements</h2>
    <ul>
        <li>PHP 8.4+</li>
        <li>OpenSwoole 26 (<code>pecl install openswoole-26.2.0</code>, or a <code>php8.4-openswoole</code> package of version 26)</li>
        <li><code>twig/twig</code> 3.10 or newer, only for Twig templates: <code>composer require twig/twig</code></li>
        <li>ext-brotli, optional: static files get Brotli level 11 once it is loaded, pages and streams with <code>withBrotli()</code></li>
        <li>A reverse proxy (Caddy or nginx) to handle TLS</li>
    </ul>

    <h2 id="systemd">systemd service</h2>
    <p>Create a service unit at <code>/etc/systemd/system/myapp.service</code>:</p>
    {% code 'ini' %}{% verbatim %}
[Unit]
Description=My php-via app
After=network.target

[Service]
Type=simple
User=www-data
WorkingDirectory=/var/www/myapp
ExecStart=/usr/bin/php /var/www/myapp/app.php
ExecReload=/bin/kill -USR1 $MAINPID
Restart=on-failure
RestartSec=5s
Environment=APP_ENV=production

[Install]
WantedBy=multi-user.target
    {% endverbatim %}{% endcode %}

    {% code 'bash' %}{% verbatim %}
systemctl daemon-reload
systemctl enable myapp
systemctl start myapp
systemctl status myapp
    {% endverbatim %}{% endcode %}
    <p>
        <code>ExecReload</code> makes <code>systemctl reload</code> send <code>SIGUSR1</code> to the OpenSwoole master, which
        restarts the workers (see <a href="#graceful-restart">Graceful restart</a>). With ext-brotli loaded, the server
        compresses the static files present at start before it opens its port, for up to 2.3&nbsp;s (see
        <a href="#static-compression">Static compression</a>), so a health check right after <code>start</code> can fail.
    </p>

    <h2 id="caddy">Caddy reverse proxy</h2>
    <p>
        Caddy handles TLS automatically, and SSE needs no setting: <code>reverse_proxy</code> flushes a
        response with <code>Content-Type: text/event-stream</code> to the client immediately, whatever
        <code>flush_interval</code> says (<a href="https://caddyserver.com/docs/caddyfile/directives/reverse_proxy#flush_interval" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Caddy docs</a>).
        A plain <code>reverse_proxy</code> streams <code>/_sse</code> as php-via writes it:
    </p>
    {% code 'caddy' %}{% verbatim %}
myapp.example.com {
    # Files in /var/www/myapp/public, linked as /public/...
    handle /public/* {
        root * /var/www/myapp
        file_server
    }

    # Proxy everything else to OpenSwoole
    reverse_proxy localhost:3000 {
        header_up X-Real-IP {remote_host}
    }
}
    {% endverbatim %}{% endcode %}

    <p>
        The live site runs this pattern: HTTP/2 cleartext from Caddy to php-via, which runs with
        <code>withH2c()</code> (see <a href="#brotli-prod">Brotli</a>) and one worker, and no flush setting.
        <code>versions h2c</code> is what an <code>h2c://</code> upstream sets, and the zero timeouts
        state Caddy's default of none, so a stream that stays open for hours is never cut. With more than
        one worker, h2c sends every client to one of them: see <a href="#tab-worker">which worker holds a tab</a>
        before you pick the upstream protocol.
    </p>
    {% code 'caddy' %}{% verbatim %}
myapp.example.com {
    reverse_proxy 127.0.0.1:3000 {
        transport http {
            versions h2c
            read_timeout 0
            write_timeout 0
        }
    }
}
    {% endverbatim %}{% endcode %}

    <p>
        Behind nginx, the <code>X-Accel-Buffering: no</code> header of every SSE response turns off
        nginx's proxy buffering for it (<a href="https://nginx.org/en/docs/http/ngx_http_proxy_module.html#proxy_buffering" target="_blank" rel="noopener">nginx docs</a>).
        Set <code>proxy_buffering off</code> for <code>/_sse</code> only if <code>proxy_ignore_headers</code>
        drops that header.
    </p>
    <p>
        An idle <code>/_sse</code> stream writes an SSE comment after 15 seconds without output, so a proxy
        idle timeout longer than that, such as nginx's default <code>proxy_read_timeout</code> of 60 seconds,
        does not cut it. If you raise <code>Config::withSseKeepAliveMs()</code>, keep it below your proxy's
        timeout.
    </p>

    <h2 id="sub-path">Sub-path mounting</h2>
    <p>
        If your app is hosted at a sub-path (e.g. <code>example.com/myapp/</code>), tell php-via
        the base path at startup with <code>withBasePath()</code>:
    </p>
    {% code 'php' %}{% verbatim %}
$config = (new Config())
    ->withBasePath('/myapp');   // also accepts '/myapp/' (trailing slash is added automatically)
    {% endverbatim %}{% endcode %}
    <p>
        Action URLs, the SSE and Datastar URLs that <code>via_head</code> and <code>via_foot</code> write, the
        {% verbatim %}<code>{{ base_path }}</code>{% endverbatim %} shell placeholder and the <code>basePath</code> Twig
        global all carry it. The value must be a relative path (<code>/myapp</code>, <code>/sub/path</code>, etc.).
        <code>withBasePath()</code> throws an <code>\InvalidArgumentException</code> for anything else, such as an
        absolute URL or a protocol-relative path. Call it before <code>new Via()</code>, which freezes the Config.
    </p>
    <p>Configure the matching Caddy block:</p>
    {% code 'caddy' %}{% verbatim %}
example.com {
    handle /myapp/* {
        reverse_proxy localhost:3000
    }
}
    {% endverbatim %}{% endcode %}

    <h2 id="brotli">Brotli compression</h2>
    <p>
        <code>withBrotli()</code> compresses pages and live SSE streams with Brotli.
        It requires the <a href="https://github.com/kjdev/php-ext-brotli" target="_blank" rel="noopener">ext-brotli</a>
        PHP extension and either <code>withCertificate()</code> or <code>withH2c()</code>, since browsers ask for Brotli
        over HTTPS only; <code>start()</code> throws when one is missing. <code>withH2c()</code> turns on HTTP/2 on the port,
        which keeps answering HTTP/1.1 too, and pages and streams get Brotli over either protocol. Static assets get
        Brotli whenever ext-brotli is loaded, without <code>withBrotli()</code> and unless <code>withBrotli(false)</code>:
        see <a href="#static-compression">Static compression</a>.
    </p>
    <p>
        Each SSE stream holds its own Brotli encoder, which grows with the traffic it carries and never
        shrinks. At the default level 4 a busy stream settles at about 9&nbsp;MB, so 2&nbsp;000 busy
        connections need about 17&nbsp;GB; an idle stream stays near 7&nbsp;KB. Level 1 uses about
        570&nbsp;KB per busy stream and a third of the CPU, for about 37% more bytes on the wire:
        <code>withBrotli(dynamicLevel: 1)</code>. Measurements are in <code>PERFORMANCE.md</code>.
    </p>
    {% code 'bash' %}{% verbatim %}
pecl install brotli
    {% endverbatim %}{% endcode %}

    <h3 id="brotli-dev">Dev mode: self-signed cert, no proxy</h3>
    <p>Generate a self-signed certificate once:</p>
    {% code 'bash' %}{% verbatim %}
openssl req -x509 -newkey rsa:2048 -keyout dev.key -out dev.crt \
    -days 3650 -nodes -subj "/CN=localhost"
    {% endverbatim %}{% endcode %}
    <p>Then configure php-via to terminate TLS directly:</p>
    {% code 'php' %}{% verbatim %}
$config = (new Config())
    ->withCertificate(__DIR__ . '/dev.crt', __DIR__ . '/dev.key')
    ->withBrotli();
// Access via https://localhost:3000 (browser will warn about self-signed cert, accept once)
// curl needs --insecure / -k
    {% endverbatim %}{% endcode %}

    <h3 id="brotli-prod">Prod mode: Caddy holds certs, php-via compresses</h3>
    <p>
        Use <code>withH2c()</code> so OpenSwoole speaks HTTP/2 cleartext (h2c) on the internal port
        while Caddy terminates TLS externally. You must disable Caddy's own <code>encode</code>
        directive. Otherwise Caddy would double-compress what php-via already compressed.
    </p>
    {% code 'caddy' %}{% verbatim %}
myapp.example.com {
    reverse_proxy h2c://localhost:3000 {
        header_up X-Real-IP {remote_host}
    }
    # No `encode` directive: php-via handles Brotli
}
    {% endverbatim %}{% endcode %}
    {% code 'php' %}{% verbatim %}
$config = (new Config())
    ->withH2c()     // enables open_http2_protocol, satisfies withBrotli(), no certs needed here
    ->withBrotli()
    ->withHost('127.0.0.1')
    ->withPort(3000);
// Caddy handles TLS; OpenSwoole speaks h2c on the internal port only
    {% endverbatim %}{% endcode %}

    <p>Compression levels:</p>
    <ul>
        <li><strong>Pages and SSE</strong>: level 4 (fast; runs in the hot path)</li>
        <li><strong>Static assets</strong>: level 11 (max ratio; never compressed in a worker's event loop, see <a href="#static-compression">Static compression</a>)</li>
    </ul>

    <h2 id="production-config">Production config</h2>
    <p>Tune php-via for production in your <code>app.php</code>:</p>
    {% code 'php' %}{% verbatim %}
$config = (new Config())
    ->withHost('127.0.0.1')    // bind locally, let Caddy handle external
    ->withPort(3000)
    ->withLogLevel(getenv('APP_ENV') === 'production' ? 'info' : 'debug')
    ->withTemplateDir(__DIR__ . '/templates')   // Twig: composer require twig/twig
    ->withStaticDir(__DIR__ . '/public')
    ->withSecureCookie(true)
    ->withTrustedOrigins(['https://myapp.example.com']);
// For multi-worker CPU parallelism, see "Multi-worker scaling" below.

$app = new Via($config);   // freezes the Config: every with* call goes before this line
    {% endverbatim %}{% endcode %}

    <h2 id="opcache-jit">OPcache &amp; JIT</h2>
    <p>
        OPcache and PHP's JIT compiler are disabled for CLI processes by default.
        Enable them explicitly in your server's <code>php.ini</code> (or via <code>-d</code> flags):
    </p>
    {% code 'ini' %}{% verbatim %}
opcache.enable=1
opcache.enable_cli=1                ; required: CLI is off by default
opcache.memory_consumption=128
opcache.interned_strings_buffer=16
opcache.max_accelerated_files=10000
opcache.validate_timestamps=0       ; set to 1 in development
opcache.jit=tracing                 ; safe with OpenSwoole; omit if no CPU-bound actions
opcache.jit_buffer_size=32M
    {% endverbatim %}{% endcode %}

    <h3 id="opcache-findings">Key findings</h3>
    <p class="note">
        Results below are from a synthetic benchmark on a single WSL2 host (PHP 8.4.20, OpenSwoole 25.2.0).
        Absolute numbers vary by hardware. Treat deltas as directional guidance, not guarantees.
    </p>
    <ul>
        <li>
            <strong>JIT tracing gives ~7.9× speedup on CPU-bound actions</strong> (tight loops, float
            arithmetic, template rendering). A Mandelbrot workload went from 349 req/s (no OPcache) to
            2,744 req/s with <code>jit=tracing</code>.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>OPcache alone doubles CPU throughput</strong> without JIT. Enabling OPcache only
            still gives roughly +120% on computation-heavy actions, with no JIT risk.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>IO-bound workloads are unaffected.</strong> If your actions mostly wait on database
            queries, external APIs, or file IO, OPcache and JIT add &lt;10%. Invest in connection
            pooling instead.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>SQLite-bound actions see modest JIT gains (+3 to 8%).</strong> Synchronous SQLite
            calls are blocking C-extension calls: the coroutine scheduler cannot yield during them,
            so 100 concurrent coroutines still serialize on SQLite regardless of JIT profile.
            Reduce query count with in-process caching (e.g. caching per-server-lifetime results
            that only change on writes) rather than relying on JIT to compensate.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>Never use <code>opcache.jit=function</code> with OpenSwoole.</strong> Function-mode
            JIT compiles <code>usleep()</code> / <code>sleep()</code> as regular blocking calls, bypassing
            OpenSwoole's sleep hook (<code>SWOOLE_HOOK_SLEEP</code>, see <a href="#hooks">Coroutine hooks</a>). This turns a non-blocking 2 ms
            yield into a thread-blocking sleep and reduces IO concurrency by ~89%.
            Use <code>jit=tracing</code> instead: it does not have this regression.
        </li>
    </ul>

    <h2 id="hooks">Coroutine hooks</h2>
    <p>
        A worker serves all its tabs from one thread, so a call that blocks the thread stops every stream,
        action and page view on that worker until it returns. OpenSwoole's coroutine hooks replace blocking
        PHP functions with versions that suspend only the calling coroutine, and the
        <code>hook_flags</code> server setting picks which ones.
    </p>
    <p>
        php-via sets <code>hook_flags</code> to <code>Via::defaultHookFlags()</code>:
        <code>SWOOLE_HOOK_ALL</code>, without <code>SWOOLE_HOOK_NATIVE_CURL</code> when libcurl is 8.20 or
        newer, where that hook crashes workers (see <a href="#hooks-bugs">the bug list</a>). The framework
        itself needs only the socket hooks, for <a href="/docs/broker#redisbroker">RedisBroker</a>: the rest
        of it uses OpenSwoole's coroutine APIs, and it reads its shell template, static files and Dev Bar
        assets once per worker. The other hooks are for your code, so that database queries, HTTP calls
        over PHP streams, file access, sleeps and shell commands in actions and views suspend instead of
        stopping the worker. Curl requests, such as Guzzle's curl handlers and Symfony's
        <code>CurlHttpClient</code>, suspend only under <code>SWOOLE_HOOK_NATIVE_CURL</code>: on libcurl 8.20
        or newer the default leaves it out, and each curl request blocks the worker until it returns.
    </p>
    <p>
        File and stdio hooks cost CPU: each hooked file call is handed to a thread pool and back, 6 to
        8&nbsp;µs per handoff, and a 91&nbsp;KB read takes 142&nbsp;µs instead of 4.5&nbsp;µs. Change the flags
        with <code>withSwooleSettings(['hook_flags' =&gt; ...])</code>, which wins over the default.
    </p>

    <h3 id="hooks-flags">What each hook covers</h3>
    <table>
        <thead>
            <tr><th>Flag</th><th>Makes these suspend instead of block</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
            <tr><td><code>SWOOLE_HOOK_TCP</code>, <code>_UDP</code>, <code>_UNIX</code>, <code>_UDG</code>, <code>_SSL</code>, <code>_TLS</code></td><td>PHP stream sockets: PDO MySQL and mysqli (mysqlnd), phpredis, predis, php-amqplib, <code>file_get_contents('https://...')</code>. Host names resolve on the thread pool. RedisBroker needs <code>TCP</code>, <code>TLS</code> with <code>tls: true</code>, or <code>UNIX</code> for a socket path.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>SWOOLE_HOOK_STREAM_FUNCTION</code></td><td><code>stream_select()</code> and <code>stream_socket_pair()</code></td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>SWOOLE_HOOK_FILE</code></td><td>Opening, reading, writing and seeking files, <code>fstat()</code>, <code>unlink()</code>, <code>mkdir()</code>, <code>rename()</code> and directory reads, each on the thread pool. <code>stat()</code>, <code>realpath()</code>, <code>include</code> and <code>fclose()</code> still block.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>SWOOLE_HOOK_STDIO</code></td><td>Every stream on a file descriptor: <code>STDIN</code>, <code>STDOUT</code>, <code>STDERR</code>, temporary files, and the pipes of <code>exec()</code>, <code>system()</code>, <code>passthru()</code>, <code>shell_exec()</code>, backticks and <code>popen()</code></td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>SWOOLE_HOOK_SLEEP</code></td><td><code>sleep()</code>, <code>usleep()</code> and <code>time_nanosleep()</code>, which become timers</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>SWOOLE_HOOK_PROC</code></td><td><code>proc_open()</code> and the other <code>proc_*</code> functions</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>SWOOLE_HOOK_NATIVE_CURL</code></td><td>All <code>curl_*</code> functions, so Guzzle's curl handlers and Symfony's <code>CurlHttpClient</code> run on the event loop. Only in an OpenSwoole built with <code>--enable-hook-curl</code>: <code>php --ri openswoole</code> then shows <code>hook-curl =&gt; enabled</code>.</td></tr>
        </tbody>
    </table>
    <p>
        <code>SWOOLE_HOOK_ALL</code> leaves out <code>SWOOLE_HOOK_BLOCKING_FUNCTION</code>
        (<code>gethostbyname()</code> and <code>exec()</code> on the thread pool), <code>SWOOLE_HOOK_SOCKETS</code>
        (ext-sockets) and <code>SWOOLE_HOOK_CURL</code>, which has no effect in OpenSwoole 26.2.
    </p>

    <h3 id="hooks-never">Calls no hook covers</h3>
    <p>These block the worker under every flag set, and every tab on it waits:</p>
    <ul>
        <li>PostgreSQL through <code>pgsql</code> or <code>pdo_pgsql</code>. OpenSwoole's own <code>OpenSwoole\Coroutine\PostgreSQL</code> client suspends; <code>php --ri openswoole</code> shows <code>postgresql =&gt; enabled</code> when the extension has it.</li>
        <li>SQLite. Short queries on a local file are fine.</li>
        <li>Extensions with their own network client, such as <code>mongodb</code>, <code>amqp</code> and <code>grpc</code>.</li>
        <li>The stat family: <code>stat()</code>, <code>filemtime()</code>, <code>file_exists()</code>, <code>is_file()</code>, <code>realpath()</code>.</li>
        <li><code>include</code> and <code>require</code>.</li>
        <li><code>echo</code> and <code>error_log()</code>. php-via's logger writes with <code>echo</code>, so a log pipe that backs up stalls the worker.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
        A hooked connection belongs to one coroutine at a time. When two coroutines use the same PDO or Redis
        connection at once, OpenSwoole stops the worker with a "bound to another coroutine" error, so give each
        coroutine its own connection or take one from a pool.
    </p>

    <h3 id="hooks-bugs">OpenSwoole 26.2 bugs and workarounds</h3>
    <table>
        <thead>
            <tr><th>Bug</th><th>Workaround</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
            <tr><td>With libcurl 8.20 or newer, the native curl hook segfaults the worker on a curl request to any host name, whether it resolves or not (<a href="https://github.com/curl/curl/issues/21558" target="_blank" rel="noopener">curl#21558</a>). IP addresses work.</td><td><code>Via::defaultHookFlags()</code> leaves <code>SWOOLE_HOOK_NATIVE_CURL</code> out on these versions, so a curl request blocks the worker until it returns. Flags you pass need the same, and <code>start()</code> logs a warning when they keep it. Guzzle's <code>StreamHandler</code> and <code>OpenSwoole\Coroutine\Http\Client</code> use the socket hooks and still suspend.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>The hooked <code>curl_setopt()</code> rejects <code>CURLOPT_PREREQFUNCTION</code>, so with <code>SWOOLE_HOOK_NATIVE_CURL</code> on, Guzzle 7.15's default client throws a <code>ValueError</code> on every request, sync and async.</td><td>Guzzle's <code>StreamHandler</code>, Symfony's <code>CurlHttpClient</code>, or <code>OpenSwoole\Coroutine\Http\Client</code></td></tr>
            <tr><td>With <code>SWOOLE_HOOK_NATIVE_CURL</code> on PHP 8.4 or newer, every request with a header callback leaks about 2.8&nbsp;KB (OpenSwoole issue #391). Guzzle's curl handlers and Symfony's <code>CurlHttpClient</code> always set one.</td><td>Recycle workers with the <code>max_request</code> setting, or use a client without curl</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>mysqli_init()</code> followed by a failed <code>real_connect()</code> corrupts the heap and crashes the worker, so a database restart takes workers down.</td><td><code>new mysqli(...)</code> or PDO MySQL, which survive a refused connection</td></tr>
            <tr><td>The coroutine <code>flock()</code> treats <code>LOCK_UN | LOCK_NB</code> as a lock request, so Symfony Lock's <code>FlockStore</code> hangs on its second acquire in a worker.</td><td>A Redis or database lock store</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>fsync()</code> returns <code>false</code> with the warning "Can't fsync this stream!" under <code>SWOOLE_HOOK_FILE</code> or <code>SWOOLE_HOOK_STDIO</code>, and the file is not synced.</td><td>Call it in a process whose flags have neither, such as <code>Via::noFileIoHookFlags()</code></td></tr>
        </tbody>
    </table>

    <h3 id="hooks-narrow">Hooks without file and stdio I/O</h3>
    <p>
        <code>Via::noFileIoHookFlags()</code> keeps the socket, stream, sleep and <code>proc_open()</code> hooks and drops
        <code>SWOOLE_HOOK_FILE</code> and <code>SWOOLE_HOOK_STDIO</code>. It keeps <code>SWOOLE_HOOK_NATIVE_CURL</code> where
        libcurl is older than 8.20 and leaves it out where the hook crashes workers; on OpenSwoole 26.2 with libcurl 8.20
        or newer it returns 1790. File and stdio calls then run on the worker's own thread: a 1.3&nbsp;KB
        read takes 2&nbsp;µs instead of 44&nbsp;µs and <code>flock()</code> 0.7&nbsp;µs instead of 28&nbsp;µs,
        <code>fsync()</code> works, and workers start no thread pool for files. A connection by host name still
        starts one for DNS.
    </p>
    {% code 'php' %}{% verbatim %}
use Mbolli\PhpVia\Via;

$config->withSwooleSettings(['hook_flags' => Via::noFileIoHookFlags()]);
    {% endverbatim %}{% endcode %}
    <p>
        It suits an app that runs no shell commands, holds no file lock across a suspension and reads only
        small local files. Under it:
    </p>
    <ul>
        <li><code>exec()</code>, <code>system()</code>, <code>passthru()</code>, <code>shell_exec()</code>, backticks and <code>popen()</code> block the worker while the command runs: a command that takes one second froze it for 1&nbsp;002&nbsp;ms, against 1.5&nbsp;ms under the default. <code>proc_open()</code> still suspends.</li>
        <li>A <code>flock()</code> held across a database query, an HTTP call or a sleep deadlocks the worker for good once a second request on it waits for the same lock: the waiting request blocks the worker, so the holder never resumes to release it. A lock around a write with nothing in between is fine.</li>
        <li>Large reads and reads from slow volumes such as NFS or FUSE block the worker: a 64&nbsp;MB read held it for 36&nbsp;ms.</li>
        <li>Writes to <code>STDERR</code> block when the pipe behind it backs up, as Docker's and supervisord's log capture can.</li>
        <li>On libcurl 8.20 or newer, curl requests block the worker until they return, as under the default. On older
            libcurl the flags include <code>SWOOLE_HOOK_NATIVE_CURL</code>, so curl requests suspend, and the other two
            native curl bugs in <a href="#hooks-bugs">the list above</a> apply.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
        Never drop <code>SWOOLE_HOOK_FILE</code> alone. With <code>SWOOLE_HOOK_STDIO</code> still on,
        <code>include</code> reads through the thread pool and suspends halfway through a file, and a concurrent
        request then fails with "Class not found". In a load test, php-via's website lost about half its requests that way.
        <code>start()</code> throws a <code>\RuntimeException</code> for that combination before it binds the
        port, and for a <code>RedisBroker</code> whose flags lack the socket hook its connection needs.
    </p>

    <h3 id="hooks-aio">Capping the AIO thread pool</h3>
    <p>
        With <code>SWOOLE_HOOK_FILE</code> or <code>SWOOLE_HOOK_STDIO</code> on, each worker starts a thread pool
        on its first hooked file call: one thread per CPU core, growing to eight per core while tasks wait
        longer than 1&nbsp;ms, with the extra threads exiting after a second idle. With <code>worker_num</code>
        at the core count, the thread count grows with the square of the cores. Cap it with
        <code>OpenSwoole\Util::setAio()</code> before <code>start()</code>:
    </p>
    {% code 'php' %}{% verbatim %}
OpenSwoole\Util::setAio(['aio_core_worker_num' => 2, 'aio_worker_num' => 4]);
$app->start();
    {% endverbatim %}{% endcode %}
    <p>
        The same keys in <code>withSwooleSettings()</code> stop the server at start-up with "Invalid server
        option". <code>setAio()</code> accepts misspelled keys without an error, so check the thread count in
        <code>/_stats</code>.
    </p>

    <h3 id="hooks-check">Checking the flags at runtime</h3>
    <p>
        <code>OpenSwoole\Runtime::getHookFlags()</code> returns the flags in effect in a worker; test one hook
        with <code>(OpenSwoole\Runtime::getHookFlags() &amp; SWOOLE_HOOK_FILE) !== 0</code>. The Dev Bar's Stats panel
        shows them wherever the Dev Bar is on. In dev mode, <code>/_stats</code> reports them under <code>runtime</code>, with the worker's thread pool
        (<code>aio_worker_num</code> threads, <code>aio_task_num</code> tasks) and its event loop lag, which
        grows when a call blocks the worker:
    </p>
    {% code 'json' %}{% verbatim %}
"runtime": {
    "hook_flags": 2147452927,
    "aio_task_num": 0,
    "aio_worker_num": 2,
    "event_loop_lag_ms": 0,
    "event_loop_lag_max_ms": 0,
    "event_loop_lag_avg_ms": 0
}
    {% endverbatim %}{% endcode %}
    <p>
        2147452927 is <code>SWOOLE_HOOK_ALL</code> without <code>SWOOLE_HOOK_NATIVE_CURL</code> on OpenSwoole
        26.2. Scripts and tests that call <code>Coroutine::run()</code> get <code>SWOOLE_HOOK_ALL</code> when
        no flags are set, and <code>Coroutine::set(['hook_flags' =&gt; 0])</code> does not turn it off; only a
        server runs under exactly the flags it is given.
    </p>

    <h2 id="security">Security hardening</h2>

    <h3 id="security-origins">Trusted origins: CSRF protection</h3>
    <p>
        By default, when no trusted-origin list is configured, php-via falls back to a
        <em>same-host</em> check: the <code>Origin</code> header of every action request must match
        the <code>Host</code> header. This already blocks cross-site requests in most cases.
        For a stronger guarantee, set an explicit allowlist:
    </p>
    {% code 'php' %}{% verbatim %}
$config->withTrustedOrigins(['https://myapp.example.com']);
    {% endverbatim %}{% endcode %}
    <p>
        With an allowlist configured, action requests whose <code>Origin</code> header does not
        exactly match one of the listed origins are rejected with <strong>403 Forbidden</strong>.
    </p>
    <p>
        A request without an <code>Origin</code> header is rejected outside dev mode, with or
        without an allowlist. Browsers send <code>Origin</code> on every POST, so this only affects
        non-browser clients (curl scripts, server-to-server calls, uptime checks). If you have such
        clients, opt in:
    </p>
    {% code 'php' %}{% verbatim %}
$config->withAllowMissingOrigin();
    {% endverbatim %}{% endcode %}
    <p>
        The Dev Bar's <code>/_via/signal</code> and <code>/_via/reset</code> and the tab-close beacon <code>POST /_session/close</code> apply the same check.
        A page served with <code>Referrer-Policy: no-referrer</code> sends its tab-close beacon with
        <code>Origin: null</code>, which is denied; the SSE disconnect then schedules the cleanup instead.
    </p>
    <p>
        Note: <code>GET /_action/…</code> always returns <strong>405 Method Not Allowed</strong>
        regardless of this setting (see <a href="/docs/actions#url">Actions → URL</a> for details).
    </p>

    <h3 id="security-cookie">Secure cookie</h3>
    <p>
        The session cookie uses <code>HttpOnly</code> and <code>SameSite=Lax</code> by default.
        Behind HTTPS, add the <code>Secure</code> flag (and the <code>__Host-</code> prefix, which
        prevents subdomain injection) by enabling:
    </p>
    {% code 'php' %}{% verbatim %}
$config->withSecureCookie(true);
    {% endverbatim %}{% endcode %}
    <p>Do not enable this in local HTTP development: the browser will silently drop the cookie.</p>
    <p>
        php-via accepts only session ids in the form it issues, 32 lowercase hex characters, and starts a new session for
        any other cookie value. Under <code>withSecureCookie(true)</code> it reads only the <code>__Host-</code> cookie. Code
        never needs the cookie: <code>$c->getSessionId()</code> and the <code>via.session</code> request attribute hold a
        hash of it, which stays the same when the cookie changes. Give the session a new cookie at login and logout with
        <code>$c->regenerateSession()</code>, or <code>$app->regenerateSession($request)</code> in middleware, so that a
        cookie someone planted or read before stops reaching the account 10 seconds later.
        <a href="/docs/middleware#session-rotation">Middleware</a> shows a login.
    </p>

    <h3 id="security-embedding">Embedding in a cross-origin iframe</h3>
    <p>
        A browser judges a cookie's same-site status against the <em>top-level</em> page, so the
        default <code>SameSite=Lax</code> session cookie is withheld when your app runs inside an
        <code>&lt;iframe&gt;</code> on a different origin. The live SSE stream is gated on that cookie,
        so the connection never attaches. <code>withEmbeddable()</code> fixes this in one call:
    </p>
    {% code 'php' %}{% verbatim %}
$config->withEmbeddable('https://parent.example');   // origins allowed to frame you (optional)
    {% endverbatim %}{% endcode %}
    <p>It sets the session cookie to <code>SameSite=None; Secure; Partitioned</code> (CHIPS, so it
        survives third-party-cookie phase-out) and, when you pass one or more origins, emits
        <code>Content-Security-Policy: frame-ancestors</code> on page responses to restrict who may
        frame the app. It implies <code>withSecureCookie(true)</code> and <strong>requires HTTPS</strong>
        (<code>withCertificate()</code>) or h2c (<code>withH2c()</code>). <code>start()</code> hard-errors
        otherwise, because a <code>SameSite=None</code> cookie without <code>Secure</code> is dropped.</p>
    <p>
        <code>frame-ancestors</code> (who may <em>frame</em> you) is unrelated to
        <code>withTrustedOrigins()</code> (which allowlists the action POST <code>Origin</code>, always
        your app's own origin). Do not conflate them: an embedded action POST still passes the CSRF
        check unchanged.
    </p>

    <h3 id="csp">Content Security Policy</h3>
    <p>
        For a policy with nonces, a global middleware creates the nonce, passes it to php-via in the
        <code>via.csp_nonce</code> request attribute and sends the header. php-via puts the nonce on every tag of
        <code>via_head</code> and <code>via_foot</code>, the import map included, and on the Dev Bar's tags:
    </p>
    {% code 'php' %}{% verbatim %}
use Psr\Http\Message\ResponseInterface;
use Psr\Http\Message\ServerRequestInterface;
use Psr\Http\Server\MiddlewareInterface;
use Psr\Http\Server\RequestHandlerInterface;

final class CspNonce implements MiddlewareInterface {
    public function process(ServerRequestInterface $request, RequestHandlerInterface $handler): ResponseInterface {
        $nonce = base64_encode(random_bytes(16));
        $response = $handler->handle($request->withAttribute('via.csp_nonce', $nonce));

        return $response->withHeader('Content-Security-Policy', "script-src 'nonce-{$nonce}'");
    }
}

$app->middleware(new CspNonce());
    {% endverbatim %}{% endcode %}
    <p>
        Datastar reads the nonce from a <code>data-nonce</code> attribute on the <code>&lt;html&gt;</code> element. With it,
        Datastar runs <code>data-*</code> expressions, the scripts of <code>execScript()</code> and <code>dispatch()</code>,
        and the reload php-via sends a tab it cannot rebuild, through script elements that carry the nonce. Without it,
        Datastar compiles expressions with <code>Function()</code>, which needs <code>'unsafe-eval'</code> in
        <code>script-src</code>, and a nonce policy blocks those scripts. The default shell and shell templates have no
        placeholder for the attribute, so write the document in a Twig layout and pass the nonce in:
    </p>
    {% code 'twig' %}{% verbatim %}
{# layout.html.twig #}
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en" data-nonce="{{ cspNonce }}">
<head>
    <meta charset="utf-8">
    {{ via_head() }}
</head>
<body>
    {% block content %}{% endblock %}
    {{ via_foot() }}
</body>
</html>
    {% endverbatim %}{% endcode %}
    {% code 'php' %}{% verbatim %}
$app->page('/', function (Context $c): void {
    $c->view('home.html.twig', fn (): array => ['cspNonce' => $c->getRequestAttribute('via.csp_nonce')]);
});
    {% endverbatim %}{% endcode %}

    <h3 id="security-devbar">Dev Bar in production</h3>
    <p>
        The Dev Bar is off outside dev mode, and its endpoints answer 404. <code>withDevBar(true)</code> turns it on in
        production, read-only: signal writes stay off whatever <code>withDevBarOptions(writes:)</code> says. Read-only does
        not mean private. Everyone who opens a page sees the traces, logs, scopes and context ids of every visitor's tabs,
        with SESSION scopes as hashes, and the server's request counts and runtime figures in the Stats panel. Its endpoints under <code>/_via/</code> do not pass through
        your middleware, so keep it off on a site where that is too much, or block <code>/_via</code>, <code>/_via/*</code>
        and <code>/_traces</code> for other visitors in the proxy. See <a href="/docs/dev-bar">Dev Bar</a>.
    </p>

    <h3 id="security-checklist">Quick checklist</h3>
    <ul>
        <li>Set <code>withTrustedOrigins([…])</code> to your production domain(s).</li>
        <li>Set <code>withSecureCookie(true)</code> when serving over HTTPS.</li>
        <li>Bind the server to <code>127.0.0.1</code> (not <code>0.0.0.0</code>) and let Caddy/nginx face the internet.</li>
        <li>Use <code>@post()</code> (not <code>@get()</code>) for all action buttons and form submissions.</li>
        <li>Call <code>regenerateSession()</code> at login and logout.</li>
        <li>Read the session id from the <code>via.session</code> attribute in middleware, never from the cookie.</li>
        <li>Leave <code>withDevBar()</code> off unless every visitor may see every other visitor's traces.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2 id="graceful-restart">Graceful restart</h2>
    <p>
        To deploy new app code without stopping the server, reload it. <code>SIGUSR1</code> to the master process
        restarts the workers:
    </p>
    {% code 'bash' %}{% verbatim %}
systemctl reload myapp          # with ExecReload in the unit above
# or, with ['pid_file' => '/run/myapp.pid'] in withSwooleSettings():
kill -USR1 $(cat /run/myapp.pid)
    {% endverbatim %}{% endcode %}

    <p>
        A stopping worker asks its tabs to reconnect at once and ends their streams, which fires
        <code>onClientDisconnect</code>. It waits for its <code>spawn()</code> tasks and then runs your
        <code>onWorkerStop</code> callbacks, exactly as on <code>SIGTERM</code> or <code>SIGINT</code>, within
        <code>max_wait_time</code> (3&nbsp;s by default). The same happens when a worker is recycled after
        <code>max_request</code>. A callback cannot tell a reload from a stop, so keep "server going down" announcements
        out of <code>onWorkerStop</code>.
    </p>
    <p>
        What a reconnecting tab gets depends on the worker count. With one worker, the records that let php-via rebuild a
        tab live in the worker's memory and go with it, so each open tab reloads its page. With several workers, the
        records are in shared memory and survive the reload, so each tab is rebuilt by
        <a href="/docs/lifecycle#revival">revival</a> and keeps the signal values it holds.
    </p>
    <p>
        A reload runs new code only for classes the master process has not loaded. php-via's own classes, and whatever
        <code>app.php</code> loads before <code>start()</code>, stay as they were: see the
        <a href="/docs/development#thin-bootstrap">thin bootstrap pattern</a>. Upgrading php-via, or changing code the
        master loaded, needs a full restart (<code>systemctl restart myapp</code>), and with several workers that also
        clears the shared tables.
    </p>

    <h2 id="multiple-instances">Multi-worker scaling</h2>
    <p>
        php-via defaults to a single worker process. That's the right choice for most deployments:
        a single worker handles thousands of concurrent SSE connections without context-switch overhead.
        Use multiple workers when you have <strong>CPU-bound actions</strong> (computation, rendering)
        and need parallelism on a multi-core machine.
    </p>

    <h3 id="same-machine">Same machine: <code>withWorkerNum()</code></h3>
    <p>
        One php-via process with several OpenSwoole workers. No external infrastructure, and no sticky
        routing: the load balancer sees one port and OpenSwoole picks the worker for each connection.
        Without <code>withBroker()</code>, <a href="/docs/broker#swoolebbroker">SwooleBroker</a> carries broadcasts
        between the workers.
    </p>
    {% code 'php' %}{% verbatim %}
$config = (new Config())
    ->withWorkerNum(\OpenSwoole\Util::getCPUNum());
    {% endverbatim %}{% endcode %}
    <p>
        Set the count with <code>withWorkerNum()</code>. A <code>worker_num</code> in <code>withSwooleSettings()</code> that
        differs from it makes <code>start()</code> throw, since php-via sets up its shared tables for the count
        <code>withWorkerNum()</code> gives.
    </p>
    <p id="tab-worker">
        A tab lives on one worker: the one that holds its SSE stream, or until the stream connects the one
        that rendered the page. A worker that gets an action or a <code>download()</code> URL of a tab it does not
        hold passes the request to the tab's worker over OpenSwoole's worker pipe and sends back that worker's
        answer, with its cookies and a rotated session cookie. Middleware and the handler run on the tab's
        worker only. Passing a request on adds about 0.04&nbsp;ms to it, and a passed action costs the server
        about twice the CPU of one that reaches the tab's worker (0.18 against 0.09&nbsp;ms in a benchmark of
        TAB actions). An upload goes over as it arrived, so <code>file()</code> and <code>move_uploaded_file()</code>
        work there.
    </p>
    <p>
        OpenSwoole hands each connection to one worker, and every request on that connection goes there. So which
        worker a request reaches depends on how the proxy talks to php-via:
    </p>
    <ul>
        <li>
            <strong>h2c</strong>, as in the <a href="#caddy">Caddy setup above</a>: every client shares the proxy's one upstream
            connection, and with it one worker, until that connection carries 1&nbsp;280 streams at once, OpenSwoole's limit
            per connection. OpenSwoole&nbsp;26.2 ignores a lower <code>http2_max_concurrent_streams</code>. Below that,
            further workers take no load and nothing is passed on.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>HTTP/1.1</strong>: the proxy opens a connection per concurrent request, and the connections spread across
            the workers. A tab's stream and its actions then travel on different connections, so with N workers about
            (N&minus;1)/N of a tab's actions reach a worker that passes them on.
        </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
        Use HTTP/1.1 when several workers are there for CPU-bound handlers. In Caddy that is a plain upstream address
        without <code>h2c://</code>. Keep <code>withH2c()</code> in the config: the port then answers both protocols, and
        <code>withBrotli()</code> still compresses pages and streams over HTTP/1.1. With one worker per server, h2c
        costs nothing.
    </p>
    {% code 'caddy' %}{% verbatim %}
myapp.example.com {
    reverse_proxy 127.0.0.1:3000 {
        transport http {
            read_timeout 0
            write_timeout 0
        }
    }
}
    {% endverbatim %}{% endcode %}
    <p>
        The stream is not passed on. When it reconnects to another worker, that worker rebuilds the context by
        re-running the route handler, as a <a href="/docs/lifecycle#revival">revival</a> does, and the worker
        that held the tab drops its copy and hands over what the browser cannot send: the scripts and
        <code>patchElements()</code> patches still queued, the TAB signal values the browser has not received,
        and the values of server-owned TAB signals. An action still running there finishes on the old copy,
        and what it sends follows the tab once it ends; a <code>spawn()</code> task still running there sends
        nothing more, as for a destroyed context. Other server-side state, such as
        <code>#[Persist]</code> properties, starts over. Scoped signal values, GlobalState, session data, tab
        state and the connected-client list are in shared memory, and <code>withActionRateLimit()</code>
        counts across workers.
    </p>
    <p>
        A worker waits up to <code>withContextTimeouts(forwardMs: ...)</code> (60&nbsp;s by default) for the tab's
        worker to answer, and then answers 504; the action may still finish, and its cookies go out with the
        tab's next action response. When the tab's worker stops or crashes after it got the request, the
        worker that passed it answers 503 and does not run the action itself, since it may have run. The
        next request of the tab finds no live worker for it, so the worker it reaches rebuilds the context
        and takes the tab. A stopping worker asks its tabs to reconnect at once, so their streams move to the workers that
        remain.
    </p>
    <p>
        Each worker rewrites the directory entries of its open streams every quarter of the directory TTL
        or of the revival window, whichever is shorter (150&nbsp;s by default). A context that does not fit
        in the directory is logged, and its actions on other workers answer 400. An action of a tab whose
        context no running worker holds any more rebuilds it on the worker it reaches, which then holds the
        tab for the reconnect timeout (<code>withContextTimeouts(reconnectMs: ...)</code>), see
        <a href="/docs/lifecycle#without-stream">Lifecycle</a>.
    </p>
    <p>
        Each worker keeps the <code>getClients()</code> list it built last, about 2.3&nbsp;KB per client,
        and rebuilds it after a client connects or leaves on any worker. A broadcast reads the list once for
        all the views it renders. A client past the capacity set by <code>withContextDirectorySize()</code>
        is left out of the list with a warning. When a worker crashes, its clients leave the list as soon
        as OpenSwoole restarts it, and every 60&nbsp;s the first worker drops the clients of processes that
        no longer exist.
    </p>
    <p>
        <strong>What stays per worker.</strong> The server logs these at start-up:
    </p>
    <ul>
        <li>
            <strong>Read-modify-write on shared values.</strong> <code>setValue($s-&gt;int() + 1)</code> and
            <code>setGlobalState($k, globalState($k) + 1)</code> lose updates when two workers read the same
            value. Use <code>Signal::increment()</code> / <code>incrementGlobalState()</code> for counters,
            <code>Signal::mutate()</code> / <code>mutateGlobalState()</code> for anything else, and
            <code>#[Signal(..., atomic: true)]</code> in the composition API. During a broadcast each scoped
            signal is read from shared memory once and reused for every tab the flush renders, so a view that
            computes a new value from one needs <code>increment()</code> or <code>mutate()</code> as well.
            Session data has no atomic update: two tabs of one session that read a key and write it back at
            the same moment can lose one write, so keep a value several tabs change at once in a
            <code>Scope::SESSION</code> signal and use <code>mutate()</code>.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>PHP statics and objects in your own code.</strong> A simulation held in a static diverges
            per worker.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>Server-owned TAB signals.</strong> When a tab's stream reconnects to another worker after
            the worker that held it stopped or crashed, the context rebuilt there starts them from the handler's
            initial value. Keep state that has to survive that in a scoped signal or in <code>tabState()</code>.
        </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
        The shared tables are sized at startup. <code>Config::withGlobalStateTableSize()</code> (default
        1&nbsp;024 keys &times; 32&nbsp;KB per value), <code>withScopedSignalTableSize()</code> and
        <code>withContextDirectorySize()</code> tune them. Row counts are a floor: OpenSwoole admits more keys
        than requested, then rejects by hash, and a full GlobalState table throws
        <code>\OverflowException</code>. GlobalState keys are at most 63 characters.
    </p>
    <p>
        The context directory keeps a row for every page view until its revival window ends. A page whose
        stream never opened, which is every crawler hit, holds its row for the connect timeout plus the
        revival window: 30&nbsp;s + 600&nbsp;s by default. Size it for peak open tabs plus peak page views
        per second &times; 630; the default 4&nbsp;096 rows (about 8&nbsp;000 in practice) covers about
        12 page views per second. When it is full, new contexts get no row and a warning is logged at most
        every 10&nbsp;s per worker; those tabs work, but their actions on other workers answer 400. With
        <code>withContextTimeouts(revivalWindowMs: 0)</code> a row holds only the tab's worker and goes when the
        context is destroyed: actions on other workers still reach the tab, but a stream that reaches another worker
        reloads the page, since no worker rebuilds a tab.
    </p>
    <p>
        Session data takes one row per session that holds data, with all of its keys, and
        <code>withSessionTableSize()</code> sizes it (default 1&nbsp;024 sessions &times; 16&nbsp;KB per
        session). A write that takes a session past its byte cap throws <code>\OverflowException</code>.
        Rows have no age limit: a session holds its row from its first write until its last key is
        cleared or it is evicted, so the count includes visitors who left long ago, not only the ones
        online now. Past the session count, the first worker drops the least recently used sessions every
        second and logs a warning, and their data is gone. Size the count for every session that stores
        data in the time you want it kept, such as a login's lifetime. Values are serialized, so they must
        be serializable and an object comes back as a copy.
    </p>
    <p>
        The session table reserves about twice the session count, rounded up to a power of two, times the
        bytes per session: 33&nbsp;MB at the defaults, 260&nbsp;MB for 5&nbsp;000 sessions. About
        9&nbsp;MB of that is resident from start-up. The rest becomes resident as sessions store data and
        stays resident after they are cleared or evicted, so set container memory limits for the full
        reservation, or lower the bytes per session. If the kernel refuses the reservation, the server
        fails at start-up.
    </p>

    <h3 id="independent-processes">Multiple independent processes (no broker)</h3>
    <p>
        Run <em>N</em> php-via processes on separate ports, each with a single worker. Processes share
        nothing, so a cookie-sticky load balancer has to route each browser session to one upstream: a
        context, its signals and its session data exist only in the process that rendered the page, and any
        other process answers <strong>400 Invalid context</strong>.
    </p>
    <p>
        <strong>Limitation:</strong> cross-process broadcasts (<code>Scope::ROUTE</code>,
        <code>Scope::GLOBAL</code>, <code>Scope::SESSION</code> across processes) are not
        supported. Use one process with several workers, or RedisBroker, if you need those scopes.
    </p>
    {% code 'php' %}{% verbatim %}
// app.php: run N copies with different VIA_PORT values
$config = (new Config())
    ->withPort((int) (getenv('VIA_PORT') ?: 3001))
    ->withWorkerNum(1); // one worker per process, no broker needed
    {% endverbatim %}{% endcode %}
    {% code 'caddy' %}{% verbatim %}
myapp.example.com {
    reverse_proxy localhost:3001 localhost:3002 localhost:3003 localhost:3004 {
        lb_policy cookie caddy_lb   # sticky: correctness-critical, not optional
        health_uri     /_health
        health_interval 5s
    }
}
    {% endverbatim %}{% endcode %}
    <p>
        Start N copies via systemd template units or a process supervisor that varies
        <code>VIA_PORT</code>. With cookie-sticky routing in place, throughput scales
        near-linearly with the number of processes up to CPU saturation.
    </p>

    <h3 id="multi-server">Multiple servers: RedisBroker / NatsBroker</h3>
    <p>
        For deployments across multiple machines or containers, use
        <a href="/docs/broker#redisbroker">RedisBroker</a> or
        <a href="/docs/broker#natsbroker">NatsBroker</a>. These carry scope invalidations over
        the network so every server reacts to changes on any other server.
        The shared memory above is per machine, so contexts, scoped signal values and GlobalState are not
        shared between servers: route each browser session to one server with cookie-sticky load balancing.
    </p>
    {% code 'php' %}{% verbatim %}
use Mbolli\PhpVia\Broker\RedisBroker;

$config = (new Config())
    ->withWorkerNum(4)
    ->withBroker(new RedisBroker($_ENV['REDIS_HOST'], 6379));
    {% endverbatim %}{% endcode %}

    <h2 id="static-assets">Static assets</h2>
    <p>
        <code>withStaticDir()</code> serves the files of a directory from the workers, out of memory and with Brotli
        level 11 (see <a href="#static-compression">Static compression</a>); a 91&nbsp;KB stylesheet costs 0.054&nbsp;ms of
        CPU per request.
    </p>
    {% code 'php' %}{% verbatim %}
$config->withStaticDir(__DIR__ . '/public');
    {% endverbatim %}{% endcode %}

    <p>A proxy can serve the same directory instead, which keeps those requests off the workers entirely.</p>
    <p>
        Leave <code>/datastar.js</code> to php-via: it serves the plain Datastar bundle, or the Rocket build
        with <code>withDatastarRocket()</code>. A proxy that serves it from disk has to pick the matching
        file, <code>datastar.js</code> or <code>datastar-rocket.js</code> from
        <code>vendor/mbolli/php-via/public/</code>. An app that
        <a href="/docs/web-components#pin-datastar">pins Datastar</a> with
        <code>getDatastarIntegrity()</code> needs a proxy that passes the file on unchanged; compressing
        it is fine.
    </p>
    <p>
        Every static response from php-via, including the framework's own
        <code>/datastar.js</code> and <code>/via.css</code>, carries an <code>ETag</code> and
        <code>Last-Modified</code>, and honors <code>If-None-Match</code> / <code>If-Modified-Since</code>
        with a <code>304</code>. A <code>HEAD</code> request gets the same headers and no body, with the
        <code>Content-Length</code> of the body a <code>GET</code> would get; over HTTP/2, OpenSwoole 26.2 leaves
        that header out. The <code>Cache-Control</code> policy on top of that follows
        <code>devMode</code> by default (<code>no-cache</code> in dev, so edits show up on the next reload;
        <code>public, max-age=3600, must-revalidate</code> otherwise) and can be overridden explicitly.
        The default policy caches <code>/datastar.js?v=&lt;hash&gt;</code> with the served bundle's hash for a
        year (<code>immutable</code>), since a new bundle gets a new URL.
    </p>
    {% code 'php' %}{% verbatim %}
$config->withStaticCacheControl('public, max-age=31536000, immutable'); // e.g. if you fingerprint filenames yourself
    {% endverbatim %}{% endcode %}
    <p>
        Or pass a closure to fine-tune the value per file. It receives the absolute file path and the
        resolved MIME type (no charset suffix, e.g. <code>text/css</code>), and returns
        <code>null</code> to keep the default policy for that file, the year for
        <code>/datastar.js?v=</code> included:
    </p>
    {% code 'php' %}{% verbatim %}
$config->withStaticCacheControl(function (string $filePath, string $mimeType): ?string {
    if (preg_match('/\.[0-9a-f]{8,}\./', basename($filePath)) || str_starts_with($mimeType, 'font/')) {
        return 'public, max-age=31536000, immutable'; // fingerprinted assets and fonts
    }

    return null;
});
    {% endverbatim %}{% endcode %}
    <p>
        A string is always taken literally, never invoked as a function name. Pass a closure, or
        first-class callable syntax (<code>$obj->method(...)</code>) for an existing method.
    </p>
    <p>
        Each worker keeps a file in memory after its first request and reads it again only when its modification
        time or size changes. It keeps files up to 2&nbsp;MiB, and up to 16&nbsp;MiB in all; a bigger file, or one
        that no longer fits, goes out with <code>sendfile()</code>. Brotli forms have their own memory, see
        <a href="#static-compression">Static compression</a>. Dev mode checks the file on every
        request, so an edit shows up on the next reload, and when the memory is used up it first drops files
        that were deleted or changed, such as earlier builds of a bundle with a hash in its name. In
        production, reload the workers after changing files (see <a href="#graceful-restart">Graceful restart</a>).
    </p>
    <p>
        A request path whose last segment has a file extension, such as <code>/css/site.css</code>, is
        looked up in the static directory before routing. A path without one, such as an ACME challenge
        token, is looked up only when no route matches, so a route and an extension-less file with the same
        path serve the route. Paths under <code>/_action/</code>, <code>/_via/</code> and
        <code>/_session/</code> are never looked up before routing, even with a dot in them. php-via
        resolves the directory's real path once per worker, so a static directory behind a symlink that a
        deployment switches keeps serving the old target until the workers reload.
    </p>

    <h3 id="static-refused">Paths never served</h3>
    <p>
        php-via answers 404 without a look at the disk for a path with a segment that starts with a dot, such as
        <code>/.env</code>, <code>/.git/config</code> or <code>/assets/.cache/app.js</code>, and for PHP sources:
        <code>.php</code> with or without a version digit (<code>.php5</code>), <code>.phps</code>,
        <code>.phpt</code>, <code>.pht</code>, <code>.phtml</code>, <code>.phar</code> and <code>.inc</code>.
        <code>/.well-known/</code> is the exception, for ACME challenges and similar. The path is percent-decoded
        first, so <code>/%2eenv</code> is refused too and <code>/my%20file.css</code> finds <code>my file.css</code>.
        A link inside the directory that points at such a file answers 404 as well. A route with such a path still
        works.
    </p>

    <h3 id="static-compression">Static compression</h3>
    <p>
        Whenever ext-brotli is loaded, php-via sends compressible static files with Brotli at level 11 to clients
        that accept it: <code>withStaticDir()</code> files of the types below, <code>/datastar.js</code>,
        <code>/via.css</code> and the Dev Bar assets. This needs neither <code>withBrotli()</code> nor HTTP/2, since
        a static response is compressed whole, but browsers ask for Brotli over HTTPS only. Each of these responses carries <code>Vary: Accept-Encoding</code>
        and the same weak <code>ETag</code> for every encoding, so a revalidation gets a <code>304</code> whichever
        form the client holds.
    </p>
    <ul>
        <li><strong>Compressed</strong>: css, js, mjs, json, map, webmanifest, wasm, svg, ico, ttf, otf, txt, md, csv, html, htm, xml, rss, atom.</li>
        <li><strong>Sent as they are</strong>: woff2, woff, png, jpg, webp, gif, avif, pdf, mp4, webm and mp3, which are compressed already, and any type php-via does not list.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
        Level 11 takes 90 to 140&nbsp;ms per 100&nbsp;KB of JavaScript or CSS, 2.1&nbsp;s for a 1.7&nbsp;MB library,
        so a worker never compresses at that level itself:
    </p>
    <ul>
        <li>
            <strong>At start</strong>, php-via compresses the files of up to 128&nbsp;KiB that exist, in the master
            process, so every worker inherits them without a copy of its own. It starts a file only while its expected
            time fits a 2&nbsp;s budget. Level 11 takes 1.3 to 2.1&nbsp;&micro;s per byte of JavaScript or CSS, and a
            file can take longer than expected, so the step ends at most one 128&nbsp;KiB file, about 0.3&nbsp;s, past
            the budget: by 2.3&nbsp;s. The port opens after this step, so a restart takes
            that much longer, and readiness checks and rolling restarts have to allow for it: the website (11 files,
            300&nbsp;KB compressed to 64&nbsp;KB) takes 0.3&nbsp;s. Dev mode skips it.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>Right after start</strong>, the first worker has a helper process compress the files left out,
            bigger files and those past the budget, up to 32&nbsp;MiB of them, and keeps the results with the files
            compressed at start. Another worker asks the helper on its first request for such a file, and the helper
            answers from the results it keeps. php-via starts the helper with <code>withStaticDir()</code> or in dev
            mode.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>Later</strong>, a file that changed, or one left out that the helper has not compressed yet, goes
            to the helper on its first Brotli request. Until
            its result arrives, a worker sends the file at level 4 when it is up to 256&nbsp;KB and uncompressed when it is bigger, with
            <code>Cache-Control: no-store</code>, so neither a browser nor a CDN keeps that stand-in. The helper
            compresses one file at a time at the lowest CPU priority, so on a core a worker needs, the worker goes
            first. An edited 91&nbsp;KB stylesheet got level 11 after 0.1&nbsp;s, a 546&nbsp;KB library after
            0.9&nbsp;s and a 2.3&nbsp;MB bundle after 3&nbsp;s.
        </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
        To skip that work for a file, ship it precompressed: <code>foo.css.br</code> next to <code>foo.css</code>.
        php-via sends the sidecar as it is when it is at least as new as the file, even without ext-brotli. That is
        the way to ship large assets:
    </p>
    {% code 'bash' %}{% verbatim %}
brotli --best public/js/app.js    # writes public/js/app.js.br; rerun after each build
    {% endverbatim %}{% endcode %}
    <p>
        Build sidecars in the deploy step, after the files are in place, and keep them out of git. A fresh clone,
        or a Docker <code>COPY</code> of one, gives a file and its sidecar the same modification time even when the
        sidecar was not rebuilt after the file's last change. php-via then takes the stale sidecar for a fresh one,
        and Brotli clients get the old content.
    </p>
    <p>
        Memory stays bounded: up to 32&nbsp;MiB of Brotli forms compressed at start, shared by all workers, which
        the first worker tops up for itself with those the helper compressed right after start; per worker up to
        16&nbsp;MiB of Brotli forms of files compressed later, 2&nbsp;MiB each; the helper keeps
        16&nbsp;MiB of results for the other workers and needs about 50&nbsp;MB more while it compresses. A file
        over 8&nbsp;MiB, or one whose Brotli form exceeds 2&nbsp;MiB, goes out uncompressed unless it has a sidecar,
        and a sidecar over 2&nbsp;MiB goes out with <code>sendfile()</code>. With the website's own files a server
        uses about as much memory as before; with three large libraries added (2.3&nbsp;MB, 1.7&nbsp;MB and
        546&nbsp;KB) it uses about 15&nbsp;MB more in all, for the helper and the forms compressed at start.
    </p>
    <p>
        Turn static Brotli off with <code>withBrotli(false)</code>, or keep Brotli for pages and SSE only with
        <code>withBrotli(staticLevel: 0)</code>, for example when a CDN compresses. A proxy in front needs no
        change: Caddy's <code>encode</code> and nginx's <code>gzip</code> leave a response alone that already
        carries a <code>Content-Encoding</code>.
    </p>

